# Caddy config for the production VPS (v2.11.4 as deployed).
#
# Under version control because the app's trust assumptions depend on it:
# app/main.py _client_key resolves the rate-limit key from X-Forwarded-For,
# and whether that header can be forged is decided entirely here.
#
# Deliberately minimal. Caddy >= 2.5 ignores incoming X-Forwarded-* values
# unless trusted_proxies is set, so with no trusted_proxies the backend
# receives exactly one entry -- the real peer -- and a client-supplied header
# is discarded at the edge. An explicit
# `header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}` would only restate that default.
#
# If a CDN is ever put in front of Caddy, trusted_proxies becomes necessary
# AND the whole incoming header is then forwarded upstream, untrusted entries
# included. _client_key reads the last entry for that case; revisit both
# together, never one alone.
#
# No `log` block: Caddy writes no per-request access log without one, and
# audit access tokens travel as ?token=... Adding access logging here means
# adding the query filter in the same change:
#
#   log {
#       format filter {
#           wrap json
#           fields { request>uri query { delete token } }
#       }
#   }

45-10-40-169.sslip.io {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000
}
